exports[`renderAuthMd > matches snapshot and omits secrets 1`] = `
"# auth.md

You are an agent. **OpenSesame** supports agentic registration:
discover → register → (claim if needed) → exchange for an access_token → call API → handle revocation.

Profile: \`workos-auth.md/v0.6.0\`. This is a WorkOS ecosystem protocol, not an IETF RFC.
Runtime metadata at the Protected Resource Metadata and Authorization Server metadata URLs
is authoritative if this file and those documents disagree.

Resource: \`https://api.opensesame.test/\`
Authorization server: \`http://127.0.0.1:8788\`

## Step 1 — Discover

A 401 from the resource includes:

\`\`\`http
WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata="https://api.opensesame.test/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource"
\`\`\`

### 1a. Protected Resource Metadata

\`GET https://api.opensesame.test/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource\`

### 1b. Authorization Server metadata

\`GET http://127.0.0.1:8788/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server\`

Read \`agent_auth\` in full. Identity types actually enabled: anonymous, service_auth.
Provider ID-JAG (\`identity_assertion\`) is not advertised and not enabled.
SET events are not advertised and not enabled.

## Step 2 — Pick a method

1. No user identity → \`anonymous\` (if enabled).
2. Email hint only → \`service_auth\` (if enabled).
3. Provider ID-JAG → not accepted unless discovery lists \`identity_assertion\`.

## Step 3 — Register

### anonymous

\`\`\`http
POST /agent/identity
Content-Type: application/json

{ "type": "anonymous" }
\`\`\`

Response (200) includes a service-signed \`identity_assertion\` (JWT \`typ: os-sia+jwt\`),
\`pre_claim_scopes\`, a one-time \`claim_token\` (\`clm_…\`), and \`post_claim_scopes\`.
Exchange the assertion at \`/oauth2/token\` immediately. The claim ceremony is optional.
### service_auth

\`\`\`http
POST /agent/identity
Content-Type: application/json

{ "type": "service_auth", "login_hint": "<email>" }
\`\`\`

\`login_hint\` is not proof of identity and is not used to look up or merge accounts.
The response has a \`claim\` block (\`user_code\`, \`verification_uri\`, \`expires_in\`, \`interval\`)
and **no** identity assertion until the user completes the ceremony.

Do not send a product provisional bearer (\`pst_…\`) or a product claim token (\`osc_clm_…\`) here.

## Step 4 — Claim ceremony

For anonymous, start a ceremony:

\`\`\`http
POST /agent/identity/claim
Content-Type: application/json

{ "claim_token": "clm_…", "email": "user@example.com" }
\`\`\`

Surface \`verification_uri\` and \`user_code\` to the user. They sign in at OpenSesame
and type the code on a page OpenSesame owns. Poll:

\`\`\`http
POST /oauth2/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=urn:workos:agent-auth:grant-type:claim&claim_token=clm_…
\`\`\`

Honor \`interval\` (5s). Outer claim window: 86400s.
On success the pre-claim access token is revoked; use the returned post-claim token.

## Step 5 — Exchange the assertion

\`\`\`http
POST /oauth2/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:jwt-bearer&assertion=<identity_assertion>&resource=https://api.opensesame.test/
\`\`\`

There is no OAuth refresh_token in this flow. Re-exchange the still-valid service
assertion. Service assertions use \`typ: os-sia+jwt\` and are not ID-JAGs.

## Step 6 — Use the access_token

\`Authorization: Bearer <aat_…>\` against \`https://api.opensesame.test/\`.

## Revocation

Credential: \`POST http://127.0.0.1:8788/oauth2/revoke\` (\`token\` + \`token_type_hint=access_token\`), RFC 7009.
Registration: owner-initiated revoke or expiry. Provider SET delivery is not enabled.

## Scopes

- pre-claim: resource:read, resource:create:temporary
- post-claim: resource:read, resource:create:temporary, project:create:temporary, claim:create
- resource-supported: resource:read, resource:create:temporary, project:create:temporary, claim:create

Effective scopes are the intersection of requested, registration-state, resource-supported,
and domain policy. A signed assertion cannot grant a scope policy denies.

## Errors

| Code | Where | What to do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| anonymous_not_enabled | /agent/identity | Use another enabled type |
| service_auth_not_enabled | /agent/identity | Use another enabled type |
| identity_assertion_not_enabled | /agent/identity | ID-JAG is not enabled |
| issuer_not_enabled | /agent/identity | Provider is not on this service's trust list |
| login_required | /agent/identity | Re-authenticate at the provider; mint a fresh ID-JAG |
| interaction_required | /agent/identity | First-link step-up; surface the claim block |
| invalid_login_hint | /agent/identity | login_hint must be an email |
| invalid_claim_token | /agent/identity/claim | Restart at Step 3 |
| claimed_or_in_flight | /agent/identity/claim | Follow the Step 3 response |
| claim_expired | /agent/identity/claim | Restart at Step 3 |
| invalid_grant | /oauth2/token | Assertion expired/revoked; re-register |
| authorization_pending | /oauth2/token | Wait \`interval\` |
| expired_token | /oauth2/token | Re-init claim or re-register |
| slow_down | /oauth2/token | Increase interval |

Existing guest product APIs remain at \`POST http://127.0.0.1:8788/v1/principals/provisional\`
(\`pst_…\` bearers). Those are not service identity assertions.

Existing RFC 8628 device authorization remains at the authorization server's /device endpoint.
Never print \`device_code\` values.

## Secrets policy

This document must never embed client secrets, private keys, bearer tokens, or reusable credentials.
"
`;
